  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how exposure to endocrine-disrupting chemicals (EDCs) can lead to broad health effects across the body, while also highlighting why these chemicals are difficult to regulate. On the left, common exposure sources are listed, including pesticides, oil and gas, contaminated food and packaging, industrial operations, cosmetic products, plastics, and fertilizer. In the center, a callout explains the main regulatory challenges: identifying and prioritizing which substances are EDCs, dealing with non-linear or non-monotonic dose responses, accounting for latent effects that may appear later, and understanding mixtures and cumulative exposures.\n\nAn arrow connects these exposures and challenges to a human body silhouette on the right, showing multiple affected organs and systems. The brain is linked to neurodevelopmental effects, the heart to cardiovascular complications, and abdominal organs to increased rates of obesity and diabetes mellitus. The reproductive tract is associated with infertility and reproductive cancers, and a bone icon indicates decreased bone health. Overall, the image conveys that EDC exposure is widespread, can affect many organ systems at once, and is especially hard to manage because its effects are complex, delayed, and influenced by combined exposures.",
